    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d44">

                <pb facs="0128" n="962" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg962"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 103.96/309a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-02-16T19:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 16, 1918, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6600. For Sheldon [from War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 108. The War Trade Board has established a committee of five to be known as
                    the Contraband Committee, which will, under the supervision of the War Trade
                    Board, handle the administrative problems in connection with the issuance of
                    export licenses in accordance with the general policies of the Board, and also
                    make such rules <pb facs="0129" n="963" xml:id="pg963"/> and regulations as may
                    be necessary to carry out the instructions of the Board. The actual licensing
                    will continue to be done as heretofore by the Bureau of Exports in accordance
                    with the regulations of the Contraband Committee. Full details follow by mail.
                    Repeat in, full to Christiania, Stockholm, Copenhagen, and The Hague.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
